Here is the full academic test-smell audit of `CatalogTest.java`.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 2           | 2              |
| **Total**| **10**      | **10**         |

1 of the 11 test methods (`addThrowsOnNegativeCount`) is smell-free.

---

## Findings by Severity

### HIGH

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic — `addConditionalLogic`

**Lines 26–36.** An `if/else` branch inside the test changes setup behavior per SKU. The `sku.equals("b")` path may mask a bug — the reader can't tell at a glance what the test proves.

```java
for (String sku : skus) {
    if (sku.equals("b")) {           // ← branching in test
        catalog.add(sku, 10);
    } else {
        catalog.add(sku, 1);
    }
}
assertEquals(12, catalog.quantity("a") + catalog.quantity("b") + catalog.quantity("c"));
```

**Refactor:** Remove branching; make setup linear and assertion per-SKU.

```java
@Test
void addMultipleSkus() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 10);
    catalog.add("c", 1);

    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(10, catalog.quantity("b"));
    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Some branch paths may never execute; aggregated assertion hides which SKU is wrong.

---

#### 2. Mystery Guest — `addLoadsFromFile`

**Lines 39–46.** Creates and reads a temp file at runtime. The test depends on `java.nio.file.Files` and the OS filesystem — it will break in sandboxed environments without file access.

```java
Path tmp = Files.createTempFile("catalog", ".txt");
Files.writeString(tmp, "x:7");
String[] parts = Files.readString(tmp).split(":");
```

**Refactor:** Inline the data; no file needed.

```java
@Test
void addBySkuAndQuantity() {
    catalog.add("x", 7);
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("x"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky in CI, couples test to filesystem, temp file may not be cleaned up.

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test — `addSleepy`

**Lines 49–54.** `Thread.sleep(200)` adds 200 ms with no purpose — `Catalog` is synchronous.

```java
Thread.sleep(200);   // ← pointless delay
```

**Refactor:** Delete the sleep.

```java
@Test
void addSingleItem() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slows the suite; if multiplied across hundreds of tests, adds minutes. Contributes to flakiness if timing ever matters.

---

#### 4. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — `addAndDoNothing`

**Lines 57–60.** Calls `catalog.add` but never asserts anything. The test passes even if `add` silently drops the item.

```java
@Test
void addAndDoNothing() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);   // no assertion
}
```

**Refactor:** Assert the expected side-effect.

```java
@Test
void addStoresItem() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — test inflates pass count and coverage while verifying nothing.

---

### MEDIUM

#### 5. Eager Test — `doEverythingAtOnce`

**Lines 63–72.** Calls 4 distinct production methods (`add`, `quantity`, `inStock`, `toString`) in one test. When it fails, the root cause is ambiguous.

```java
catalog.add("a", 1);
catalog.add("b", 2);
catalog.add("c", 3);
catalog.quantity("a");
catalog.quantity("b");
catalog.inStock("c");
catalog.toString();
assertEquals(6, catalog.quantity("a") + catalog.quantity("b") + catalog.quantity("c"));
```

**Refactor:** Split into focused tests, each exercising one behaviour.

```java
@Test
void addAccumulatesQuantity() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 2);
    catalog.add("c", 3);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(2, catalog.quantity("b"));
